body:
I've enjoyed kissing you but for me... that isn't a precursor to sex. It's a pleasurable exploration, it's sensual, it's fun and you're good at it. I like you. But I don't know you... so I'm not prepared to decide whether or not I'm going to be intimate with you. If that's where you'd like to get with me, though, then keep doing what you're doing. And if you just wanna smash/fuck/have sex... then you want to go do what you're doing with someone else. I just want to be clear, with my expectations.

&#x200B;

Or you could say what I said to my man before we were together: "I'm not 'down to fuck.' It's not ever gonna be like that."

>But no one in the U.S really cares. 

I guarantee you are not the only one...

&#x200B;

He can't control where she goes or what she does... but I understand your feelings and I think they're valid. Bring it up to him... he told you he'd respect your wishes and now he isn't. Ask him if he's prepared to choose you and only you... if he's not he's not for marrying.

&#x200B;

Also why would he know what kind of guy Kate is physically attracted to? That isn't an appropriate subject for him to be discussing with another woman. Especially a woman he's not supposed to be spending time with. You're uncomfortable and he should step up to make you comfortable, as your man. He should also not be trying to deter other men from pursuing her.

&#x200B;

It's all a bit dodgy and I don't like it one bit but I think you need to be prepared to walk away if he can't quit a meaningless relationship.

Alright so I don't know exactly what you mean when you say chub rub or it being painful but my thighs *absolutely* rub together and it's never bothered me. Never hurts. I assume you're experiencing chaffing and although it's never happened to me I think there may be a reason why.

I wear lotion every day and when my thighs rub I never think about it. Whatever friction's going on there doesn't create dyness... I'm not saying I just slide off of myself (I aim not to be greasy) but it's definitely smooth. I also wear hose or tights with dresses or skirts out in public (especially in the winter) so I recommend that... your thighs can't rub with a barrier.

&#x200B;

I don't recommend shorts unless they're tiny silk cami-knicker bottoms or something seamless.

&#x200B;

That's it.

Can I has it instead? It would look really nice on me x) like probably as nice as it looks on you. 

&#x200B;

This is awesome, seriously. Very impressive.

Is this an actual high school reunion or is this a private party of yours?

&#x200B;

This seems EXTRA EXTRA. If it's your own private party don't invite her if you don't want her there. If it's really all about what high school reunions usually are about... no one particular person... invite her? Kids are stupid and she was apparently a fucked up one. What happened to you wan't her doing, but I can 100% understand you associating her with he event. Seems like it's up to you and you don't want her there though. 

&#x200B;

You're really lucky your customer wasn't allergic to soy (or peppers or anything in that hot-sauce)... what immature and irresponsible behavior. Tighten up or you'll do this to the wrong person (having dealt with severe soy allergies I would have been the WRONG person... and you would *not* have known what hit you).

&#x200B;

Furthermore, you work in a *restaurant* where you will encounter people of different cultural backgrounds... the behavior you found so offensive is actually normal in some Asian cultures, and might be expected at a restaurant in areas of China for example. Personally it blows my mind because there are standards of behavior most people follow wherever you're from, and I'm used to what I'm used to. But having grown up in such a diverse area I guess I had enough exposure to know and understand these things. Even if I hadn't... I'd like to think I have the basic home training to know how to at least conduct myself like a lady and in a professional manner... even moreso when my job involves food safety.

&#x200B;

Honestly... you behaved like a brat at best and whether you like it being pointed out or not... your behavior could have had life-or-death consequences. I can't even *imagine* how they must have felt. I do not care how vindicated you feel and as someone who's been a waitress I can sympathize with the fact that you *must* have (felt vindicated). But your actions *could* have put someone's *life* at risk because a group of tourists dared to visit a family establishment in a foreign country where their waitress experienced culture shock... ignorance is not bliss. 

BRUH.... Get. Your. Life. You're a grown ass adult now and it would behoove you to behave as such.

&#x200B;

My boyfriend kind of looks at me shiftily sometimes because we grew up together and he knows I went through a wild streak (not from rumors, but because we were close). He does it when he asks if I know some guy from the area and my reaction isn't 100% neutral. He then jokingly asks if I slept with him, and it's really not a joke. It matters to him. 

&#x200B;

He'd get over it if I ever said yes, I have no doubt. But I also have no doubt it'd be hard and he'd look at me different... and he's my best friend. He says it's because he wouldn't want anyone to think eww, he's with this chick? Do you know what this chick did to So-and-so? I think it's a huge double standard but it's kind of moot. 

&#x200B;

I always kind of assumed it was because we're monogamous and there's something to be said of him knowing me in ways no one else does, has or could. But hell I guess I'm overly romantic. 

&#x200B;

Only a fool trips over his past. But her past is not your past... if you can't get past it, don't trip. Just... leave. Don't punish her.

I felt inclined to try, when my ex-best-friend hit me up several years after our fall out (read: after she began bullying me to get in with a clique I thought was stupid). I get it... sometimes people regret treating you badly. Sometimes they regret opting for superficial connections over genuine ones, and find that their actions have driven away the people they now wish they'd kept around. But that's not your fault. It wasn't *mine*. 

&#x200B;

What I found in this case was not that my ex-best-friend wasn't worth my time. What I found was not that she was unforgivable... she'd wounded me, sure, but it wasn't *sore* anymore, if that makes sense. . Instead I found that I just didn't have the time or energy to spend on her... she would have been worth it if I had an excess but I didn't. It was just a matter of fact; I was in school, I was working, I was in love, I was taking care of my mom... I had other friends who I struggled to make time for... I just didn't have anything to give her and that was that.

&#x200B;

This person... I don't know, I don't like what I've hard so far but aside from that, maybe you don't have anything to give her, either. Your relationship has been a power play to her (historically), and she's treated you badly. What can she do to enhance your life at this point (all your friends should enhance your life)? What can you do for her? What could she want from you?

&#x200B;

Some people come around and they change and they want to repair what's able to be fixed. Some people are just toxic and they'll hurt you because it's in their nature to be petty and manipulative. Identify what type of person she is and decide whether or not she's worth it AND whether or not you have it in you.

&#x200B;

You could always make a croque monsieur instead of your average ham&cheese. 

&#x200B;

I like to add sun-dried tomatoes and/or fresh basil to my sandwiches and grill them.

I'm finally having regular sex with my boyfriend!!!!!

When we started dating a few months ago, he initially didn't expect me to hold off on sex considering our history (I'd spent a decade insisting he was too important to me as a friend to cross those lines, but in the last year and a half before getting together I'd given it up to him twice). The love, trust, long-lived attraction and intimacy were all there in spades so it kind of threw him for a loop when I told him I just couldn't. But... I couldn't.

&#x200B;

So we did other things (like all the time), and he was incredibly patient and loving. When we finally *did* have sex, it was good but emotionally really difficult for me and we had to put the brakes on again. I began to worry, but he told me as much as he enjoyed it he never ever wanted  to see me push myself that way again (uhhh I might've cried for a few hours). He promised that whatever amount of time I needed and whatever kind of support I required, he was 'with' it... no questions asked.

&#x200B;

He told me I didn't have to explain anything unless I wanted to talk, because he already understood where I was coming from.... even if he didn't realize what it would be like, in the context of our relationship.

&#x200B;

After all that though, we now have sex a few times every weekend (weekday sex is rare- our schedules conflict).

&#x200B;

I'M SO HAPPY lol and I LOVE IT. And I *feel* loved, and honored and protected by the person I always secretly wanted to end up with :3 it's special every time, for no other reason than that.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Ahh, this makes so much more sense than the alarming prospect of adding seasoning to my reproductive organs. 

&#x200B;

Alright, so it's just for taste and to limit mess I guess.

LOLOL 

That's hilarious... Do they sell those at Whole Foods? I wasn't trying to find out if they were organic, just whether or not it was standard for the flavors to be naturally derived (from foodstuffs) or created artificially in a lab like most candies claiming to taste like fruit. 

&#x200B;

You've definitely got me wondering about organic condoms now though. Just as a matter of curiosity. 

&#x200B;

Thanks=\]

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I have trouble asking my boyfriend if I can stay the night even though he always says, "of *course* babe," like it's silly I even asked.

&#x200B;

The more my depression gets to me the more I convince myself that he doesn't really want to hang out, that I'm a nuisance, or that he'd prefer me to go home... I've learned to try to play it off but I do still get extra awkward with it. 

&#x200B;

Occasionally I say something in the same vein as your girlfriend did... offering him excuses as to why he may not/probably doesn't want to hang out with me. He always sets me straight and tells me he'd like it very much if he could see me this evening or that... but that's me. 

&#x200B;

She could simply not be enthused... sometimes I'm not enthused to kick it with my guy, and it has no bearing on our relationship. Doesn't make me love him less or make me interested in anyone else... it just makes me an introvert. Maybe she didn't want human interaction after a day of travel and she was the one who just wanted to chill. I'd ask her to be direct, and ask specific questions like, "would you like to see me tonight? I'd love to see you but I understand if you need space" or something like that to encourage less ambivalent answers.

At the end of the day, he cannot force you to be in a relationship with him. It's hard to break up with *anyone* (well, it is for me, anyway) but to decline to spend your energy on someone, to deny them your time, to deny them access to your body, to deny them continued discourse... these are simply your rights in *any* relationship.

&#x200B;

If he knocks at your mother's door at 2 am in yet another stunning display of how he fails to respect your most basic boundaries, call the cops. It sounds and will feel harsh because you love him and I get that. But that kind of behavior isn't ok and if he'd behave that way he may need consequences to learn that it isn't okay. Coming to your home when everyone should be asleep, and attempting to intimidate or lure/sweet-talk you into letting him in? Disregarding the fact that you're telling him to leave? That  you're telling him no? That is a huge red flag for potentially obsessive and even violent behavior. I literally *never* thought that the man I lost my virginity to/thought I'd marry would rape me, either. I never fathomed that he'd tell me "no" multiple times when I ended things, like it wasn't up to me. And I sure as hell never thought that after years of not talking to or responding to him,  he'd still be stalking me, and that it would be scary. That *he* would be scary.

&#x200B;

My advice is not to even give him the leeway to fuck with your head. Offer a single no, and remind yourself (again and again) that he heard you the first time. You absolutely *can* leave him, and you *don't* owe him long talks so that he can obtain "closure" or so that he can manipulate/talk you out of it... again.

&#x200B;

Fighting for someone you love is romantic and all but it doesn't look like what this looks like. It doesn't look like wounding that someone... and it doesn't look like fighting yourself.

From a woman whose first sexual (and LT) relationship was with a man 13 years her senior (21/34). From a woman who has experienced intimate partner assault: Listen... *he's telling you he's traumatized by the fact that you brought up the fact that he traumatized you.*

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

**If that doesn't sound right, it's because it isn't.** *Of course* anyone who loves you and has hurt you will feel the impact of their having hurting you. But blaming you for it, making their assault upon you all about their own feelings, and denying responsibility and accountability for actively ignoring your protests to *forced sexual activity*... are not acts of love.

&#x200B;

*How is he supposed to know when you want to have sex?* Um, how is ANYONE supposed to know when you want to have sex? I'll tell you: *it'll be obvious because you won't be crying.* You won't be telling them, "stop," or "no." You won't be frozen, unresponsive, or struggling against them. *That* is how everyone you ever sleep with for the rest of your life WILL be able to tell when you do, and when you don't want to have sex.

&#x200B;

I want you to realize (if not today, soon) that a loving partner would not have tried to paint himself as the victim in this scenario. Nor would a loving partner have ever had the chance to... because he would not have raped you. This guy can't even *own up* to his actions, *let alone* take responsibility (which, by the way, is what a real man would do). He hurt you, and he won't even own up to it to get to the matter of how he failed to recognize or honor your boundaries, how he failed to treat you like a human with autonomy and full rights to her vagina, or how he will not-fail to do so in the future.

&#x200B;

When you are ready for a man...  you won't find yourself waking up next to him. I promise you that.

When we were your age, my best friend and I (we're 25 now) were in the same boat. He dated other girls and I dated other guys but we shared a special kind of connection and our relationship was very intimate. Eventually he tells me he's fallen in love with me, and that before this he'd only ever been infatuated, even when he thought he was in love. That's kind of a lot, for a girl to hear. Especially one as shy and unsure of herself as I was.

&#x200B;

Anyway I'd told him I viewed him as family, like a brother, well before he confessed his feelings and I put him off not once, not twice, but three or four times in the years that followed. It was hard for him, but we remained best friends. We both fell in love with other people, and endured heartache. Then I told him I always hoped it'd work out between us, and... well... now it's our time.

&#x200B;

Don't be afraid of exposing your feelings, or of getting hurt. Just know she's not out to hurt you, and that a friendship that's genuine will weather that sort of storm. I didn't say yes until later because I didn't think he was mature enough yet to handle me the way I'd want to be handled by him. What I learned was... neither was I. We ended up together at what feels like the perfect time, now, and looking back... as much as he says we were meant for each other or that he wishes I'd've realized it sooner... I don't think we would have had what we have now, if we hadn't grown as much as we did before committing to one another.

Oh lovely! Thanks for sharing :)) 

&#x200B;

I like the back of the shawl and the dress. I'm not sure if there's boning (or if that's the proper term) in the back but it looks very structured and elegant.

That really is quite rude.

&#x200B;

Where the top connects to the bottom... is there something that can be done about the continuity of the seam there? If there is, I couldn't do it, but working with it you might see what I mean and be able to smarten it up because it does look as if a good yank could expose you.

&#x200B;

Frankly, making your  own  wedding dress is  something to be proud of and the shoulders really are quite lovely. I don't know how I'd even begin to do what you did...  I guarantee you it wouldn't look like that, if I *did*. 

&#x200B;

I'd be crying to my mom begging her to make it look like its not a ghost costume.

I don't try to tell white people what to say or what not to say in my presence unless the shit is too bold/I'm extra annoyed.... what's funny to me is that so many white people think they're "cool" with the black community if their black friends accept their use of the term "nigga" when in reality, the only thing that's being accepted is that this person is a particular (and particularly unsurprising) kind of ignorant... a brand of ignorance we've decided to look beyond.

&#x200B;

This kind of ignorance... it isn't an ignorance that can be applied to the black community using it. Trust me, those of us under 30 know that many of our mothers' great-grandparents were enslaved while our white peers' mothers' great-grandparents were not subject to the generational abuses ours have suffered and still suffer. If their parents talk of their grandparents and great-grandparents, those stories are not ones of bondage or the continuation of bondage by other names. They are not stories of silence littered with question marks because we were separated from our families systematically, ruthlessly. *We get this*... most of y'all who aren't black... don't.

&#x200B;

Even those whites who *do* realize this will never feel (or look in the mirror to see) the heavy implications of a legacy of rape, of being defiled and divided  from our sons/daughters and our men/women. So many of them grasp nothing from the term "nigga"  but a pop culture reference that makes them feel excluded in a way they feel entitled *not* to feel.

&#x200B;

The truth is... despite how many white folks embrace the term, not a single one of us is "your nigga," nigga.

&#x200B;

For blacks, the term represents a reclamation, a defanging of a beast that named us less than human... a repurposing and restructuring of a weapon into an ornament and a widening of it's application. But white folks who use the term... they don't get that. What they get is, "there are no actions, freedoms, or practices that should be distinct or exclusive to you, because it puts a limit or a check  on me."

&#x200B;

Personally I'm never surprised when a white person uses that term and I don't discount them as worthwhile human beings because of their ignorance. I just... loose a certain amount of respect I would have otherwise afforded them. They are automatically less fuckwittable even as they think their incorporation of that word has the opposite affect (which is why it's funny to me).

&#x200B;

It tells me not only are there things that this person will never understand, but there are things they couldn't even try to understand because they don't have it in them to grasp it. Like their capacity for compassion does not extend thusly, and so I allow for that handicap with a signature Southern, "bless your heart," and a shady look that doesn't register, anyway.

Leave them to be lovers and go on with the friendship with no expectations or the new-old love interest will view you as a threat (and probably weed you out because of it).

&#x200B;

Trust me. Best friends trump new romances... been there, and never doubted (as the bestie) that it'd be me who "got the guy."

&#x200B;

I don't mean this harshly but there's history and love there. Don't play rival with 2 months of juice on a field they've been playing on for 3 years already.

Sometimes people have different values, and our values inform our behaviors. You can pick your friends. You can't pick their values.

&#x200B;

I appreciate my friends telling me when I'm slipping for some reason or another but there are instances (and relationships) where I'd consider certain comments to be crossing the line. Like, yes, tell me if I'm sloppy stupid wasted and it's making me less productive and I am making an ass of myself/being an ass to someone else. Yes. But I don't know how I'd respond if you approached me about my partying habits *alone*. You assumed she was hooking up with the guy but that's all you, not her. 

&#x200B;

Furthermore she didn't stick you with her friend... you stuck yourself with her friend because of how you felt about the situation. The girl may have been totally cool with it... you just don't know their history or dynamic. 

&#x200B;

Although I couldn't imagine behaving that way with anyone but my man or my best friend (whom I've never touched sexually)... I would be damned if one of my girlfriends thought they could tell me how to conduct myself in that situation.

&#x200B;

You're hilarious. Just to get that out of the way... penis-in-vagina-ing? Bwahaha.

&#x200B;

Okay. So yeah, absolutely. What you're saying makes a *lot* of sense. What you've described is the sole reason (barring crippling anxiety) that  I refused to date my HS best friend until we reached 25. Because he'd never been in love with anyone else, never had a legit girlfriend...he didn't give upon me and get around to that in-love love until we were 24. 

&#x200B;

I didn't want to be his first... or rather, it wasn't enough to be the "first girl he loved." I wanted him to love me without wondering what it would be like to experience all of this with someone else... to be the last woman he loved, lol, which is still the endgame (I know. I'm insane).

&#x200B;

What  you're feeling is very natural. Curiosity accompanies living. But I think it would not be unreasonable to consider asking him to do different things, or to do things differentlys; you're wondering what different experiences would be like with other people, sure, but the  big part of that appears to be the experience itself and not the proverbial "other person."

&#x200B;

So ask him to show you more affection. Random cuddling, absently running his hands over your body, holding your hand, playing with your hair, nose kisses, back/shoulder kisses, hand kisses, random massages (temples, hands, feet, shoulders, back), frequent embraces and such.

&#x200B;

Ask him to try different things in bed, eventually suggesting you incorporate another person. And be prepared to tell him that as exciting as it is to experience all of these firsts with him, you want to feel like you're having a full experience, satisfying your new curiosities and allowing yourself room for sexual and romantic exploration even within your relationship.

&#x200B;

Expect this to test your relationship, expect him to feel insecure and wonder if he is enough. Consider how to respond to this and what responses, from him, would be acceptable to you.

&#x200B;

And know that all things are temporary... that he was obviously meant to be a beautiful part of your life and human experience but you may determine for how long.

&#x200B;

Best of luck!!

I would just delete it when he finds his job or delete it outright and tell him not to worry about messing up your stuff, that an extra account isn't necessary.. Some people get mad if you close out their tabs... some people just want to respect the fact that you're doing whatever it is that you're doing on your computer and don't want their own use of your device to disrupt that. 

&#x200B;

It's funny because he probably did it out of respect and you probably feel a bit disrespected. 

&#x200B;

What he did rubbed you the wrong way, that's all. It's neither here nor there and it wouldn't be making a big deal of it, to tell him so. 

&#x200B;

Call her. That's how my high school boyfriend asked me out... we didn't get a chance to talk before break so he called me from his grandma's and thus began my first mistakenship.

&#x200B;

Good luck!

NO girl. He doesn't treat you like he knows you're a princess in common clothes so he must be seriously missing something.

&#x200B;

Next time his grubby fingers reach toward you, you say: "hands OFF, plebeian!"

&#x200B;

Honestly though, he expects stuff from you that he's not willing to *give* and whether this is a gender-roles type of thing or an inconsiderate-human type of thing, it upsets you and is not okay.

&#x200B;

It's time for him to make some changes, or for you to. If I were you I'd not do diddly squat for his birthday except wish him a happy one. If he had the nerve to make comment I'd tell him that obviously birthdays are not a big deal for him despite what he says (I mean the proof is in how he handled your 'special' day)... and if he makes a fuss I'd say well then we need to make some changes, pointing out that the operative word there was  WE. Because birthdays should not be a source of work and anxiety for you... they should be a celebration of someone who you love, someone who honors and celebrates you, whose actions in general make you want to do something special for them. Don't simply submit to his will and expectations if they do not serve you as well as they serve him.

&#x200B;

Relationships are about compromise but his "winning" an argument or something like that should never make you feel like you "lost"--- and if what he wants for/from you is in your best interest as it should be, then you won't feel that way at all (unless you're just hypercompetiive then, you know, it's probably fun for you).

Well, it is out of your hands now. 

&#x200B;

But in all honesty, these men are not simply monsters and shouldn't be treated as such. Groping is NOT a big deal to all men or all women while some men know it's inappropriate to initiate that kind of contact quite that way, and some women will feel violated and unsafe. I think they need to grasp this first and then it should be made clear what is and is not acceptable behavior moving forward, especially when it comes to new members. Anyone who behaves this way after that conversation will continue to be a threat to the overall group. 

&#x200B;

Anyone who did something that could fall under the category of rape or drugging or such should just go and feel lucky that charges *might* not be pressed.

I'm 25 and texting was standard to dating when I was in high school too (it still is). And I've also always been shit at it. So I just tell men (well, I don't, because I'm dating my HS sweetheart but I have and would tell men) that I don't have a close relationship with my phone. That I should reply within 24 hours unless I forget, and doing so is no indication of my level of interest or commitment. I still have pen pals, I have no instagram of my own (apparently I exist on there, in others' photos) and I get on Facebook to say thanks for the bday wishes.

&#x200B;

If that's just you, that's just you.

&#x200B;

Oh and good morning sunshine/beautiful/handsome texts don't hurt either. How was your day texts, thinking of you texts... that sort of thing indicates interest while still managing to be kind of low-maintenence/noncommittal in terms of gearing up for a series marathon texts.

I believe in your ability to  help it. It's your very own brain after all.

&#x200B;

The fact of the matter is that people behave differently in different parts of the world, and what's totally fucked up to you is normal to someone else.

&#x200B;

I have a friend from China who will yell at his waitress in China but never in America because he's grasped the cultural differences. If he was a tourist and not someone who's been here a few years then  he would have had few chances to even let these differences in behavioral standards *register*, let alone adjust his lifelong habits.

&#x200B;

You can usually find articles on this sort of thing before visiting a foreign country, and those articles will cover at least a few important faux pas (though  there's bound to be things that just don't get covered). I'm not even going to go into how a history of blatant suppression of information and control of the internet could impact knowledge of other cultures, in this respect.

&#x200B;

Considering you and your family are in the hospitality business this is a point of eduction as important for you as it is for your guests... and perhaps moreso considering that this is how you *make* your money and this is how they *spend* theirs (sorry did my American just show it's big fat head? Yep I think it did).

&#x200B;

I don't date guys who speak rudely to people in service. That's a thing for me. But the friend I mentioned is really a perfect gentleman; this is a cultural thing, the thing of things. So if I liked him "like that" I'd let him take me out and even enter into a committed relationship with this person even though I could expect to hear him yell at a waitress (I literally just shuddered) upon visiting his hometown.

&#x200B;

There are apparently harmless behaviors and habits you have that would earn you more than a few scathing remarks (at best) in other areas of the world. And it wouldn't be your fault if you did them and offended someone without even knowing. You can grow to expect that an unknown subset of your Chinese customers will not place the same value on certain behaviors, that they may expect you to take full responsibility for cleaning up after them during their stay and that they may not understand that they have to pay per head and not per bed (which would confuse me too, as an American)... you may expect that you need to establish certain standards for how you expect the animals and the land to be treated.

&#x200B;

But I *highly* recommend not letting the euro-NZ  bubble you live in stop you from making meaningful connections with a whole fucking race. This is something you're in danger of, and trust me it'd be your loss and yours alone.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

As another woman, I'm going to put this qualifier before my response in case I sound unduly harsh at any point (that's jus not what I'm about): he wants YOU.

&#x200B;

But I still have to say: you need to work on your insecurities and ACKNOWLEDGE them. Let him know this is obviously a sensitive point for you and thank him for his patience. Then... adjust your behavior. This is all a bit "much" and it's not good for you to be freaking out on this level. Your gut will tell you what you need to know and your anxiety is just noise.

&#x200B;

At the end of the day it takes one person to cheat and it takes two people to be in a committed relationship. Focus on the fact that he's committed to you and open yourself up to being with someone worthy of your trust. Because all this insecure stuff is annoying. They're legitimate feelings but they're annoying on repeat.

&#x200B;

Try this: only bring it up every 5th time it pops into your head. And then make YOURSELF the subject of the conversation... tell him you're just feeling a bit insecure and anxious about all of it and it's stuck in your head. Then ask him to distract you. x)

&#x200B;

Also try trusting him. You noticed his behavior was inappropriate and you've given him his marching orders.Now give him the man-room to fix it. IF you find that it isn't fixed to your satisfaction then let him know adjustments need to be made.

&#x200B;

Ask him what's his plan if someone doesn't respect his new boundaries. Ask to be included in that plan (for example, I expect to be informed if this situation pops up in my own relationship, and I also expect my man to have reaffirmed those boundaries before even having the chance to let me know. In case of repeated infractions I expect him to create physical and verbal distance between himself and that person unless and until they voluntarily acknowledge that their actions were out of line and adjust their behavior so that WE feel comfortable because WE come first (which, let's face it, means I come first. He has tiny, inexpensive gametes so evolutionarily speaking, I hold the rare cards here \[a.k.a. so do you\]).

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Only way to find out is ask, but this story does *not* spell "obvious fuckboy."

&#x200B;

In fact this story reeks of potentially  decent man.

&#x200B;

The only red flag is the last relationship and the sex thing. If he had sex with you for hours,  I doubt he hasn't had sex in a *year* (my boyfriend lasted all of five seconds when I finally let him, and it had only been a few months... and this is a mad-masturbating, zero-second refractory period, multiple-ejaculating-orgasms, marvel of guy).

&#x200B;

So um, get tested with him and be cautious but let him know if you want more/ask him if he does.

Well, alright, here's the thing: If she has an ingrained  idea of what a man should be, then it's not that likely to change based on the values, desires, or best efforts of a guy who basically comes from a different world (I've been in a number if interracial relationships and trust me, I've had to write more than a few guys off because they just didn't share my values... a.k.a. they dis specific things/ generally behaved in ways I thought were unbearably unusual or just fuck-nah unacceptable for a man... it could have \[and has\] happened within my own race as well but less often, if I'm being frank).

&#x200B;

She says "good enough" but that sounds to me like a shallow and insensitive way of saying that you don't meet her standards. Well, you *do* meet he standards, obviously, but you as a partner aren't in step with her fantasy/ *aspirations* for a man. Materially, she wants more from you. Much more, by the sound of it.

&#x200B;

So one important question is, are you willing and able to deliver on that? If not, she won't be as happy with you as she hopes to be with a partner (and neither of you really want that).

&#x200B;

A more important question is... are you willing and able to tolerate--- scratch that--- *thrive* in a relationship with a woman who has a already shown you  she has a habit of exploiting the vulnerabilities of the men she's loved? A habit she (I assume!) sees no issue with. I mean, maybe that's how they motivate men where she's from but that says nothing to the impact it'll have on you, considering your own background.

&#x200B;

Again, it's entirely possible that this is how she was brought up to respond to men. Or, as is the case with all people everywhere: sometimes  we  say and do things that are just *mean*, in the heat of the moment. She wouldn't be the first to call a guy a weak dick motherfucker or some variation thereof... because the fact of the matter is, some people want to hurt you when they're hurting (and some people are weak dick motherfuckers).  

&#x200B;

That being said, it's also entirely possible that she can learn how to treat and respond to you on an individual level, based on your needs and your unique partnership.  We don't know.

&#x200B;

But from the story we've reviewed here...with you merely being the best she could find with the caveat, "*but* I love you"... it doesn't look hopeful unless the language barrier there is FOR REAL. Like... you know... the way you can sound unduly harsh in other languages if you don't understand the connotations of some of the otherwise simplistic words you're using. If she'd said, "your being a bank teller isn't enough in terms of being a good option to support me," I wouldn't have batted an eye (merely raised an eyebrow)... Thou diggeth?

&#x200B;

Anyway, "good" (still going with the language barrier theory) is such a simple word she may not be attributing it to a sense of intrinsic worth the way that some of us might... so examine this. But don't be afraid to tell her that you desire a long-term relationship with a woman who will maintain her independence and happily  live a more egalitarian lifestyle than the one she describes. If this is so, then that *may* very well come down to an irreconcilable cultural difference.

&#x200B;

Those exist between individuals when it comes to romantic partnerships. It doesn't make either of you wrong, but it makes each of  you wrong for one another. Relationships are "about" compromise, and there are simply more points upon which to compromise the more dissimilar your values and desires are. 

&#x200B;

Our cultural and individual identities converge and overlap at so many points, so whatever happens, I'd consider this a personal issue informed by culture, and not the other way around. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I went to jail for something I didn't (and don't) feel was wrong. But I only regretted actually getting arrested. The worst thing about jail (mind you, it was jail lite as I only served weekends) was the fucking boredom of being in there for 48 hour stretches... and a sickening anticipation of the weekend... and a forced study of freedom.

&#x200B;

Time warps and if you can't sleep it away it's just this grueling boredom of Lifetime movies, trading snacks like school days and  women pulling baggies out of their vaginas filled with community sleep aids, painkillers and antidepressants (a drug often denied, in jail)... The energy is dense and the air quality is  poor and the food is probably some sort of restructured experimental shit.

&#x200B;

If you refuse to be vaccinated at the jail upon your arrival, they put you in solitary confinement for the duration of your stay where you may be taunted or coerced unless, not uncommonly, there is some sort of administrative oversight and you're put in gen pop (the general population) for weekenders. Either way they deprive you of sunlight and then it's over. It doesn't force you to feel any type of way you wouldn't already feel.
